es here.","confidence":"title-only"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"W000806","score":0.09237866433913529},"overlapCount":75},"meaning":{"percentile":66.13489180224293,"median":0.010636331736930488,"n":6331,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.7093586824790074}},{"billId":"119-hr-1703","bioguideId":"B001327","cycle":2026,"score":0.008040986131800789,"matches":[{"industry":"insurance","donorAmount":45500,"donorShare":0.013893602620136806,"billConfidence":0.7,"contribution":0.004631200873378935},{"industry":"health-providers","donorAmount":37000,"donorShare":0.011298094438353007,"billConfidence":0.5,"contribution":0.0026900224853221443},{"industry":"pharma","donorAmount":5500,"donorShare":0.0016794464705659875,"billConfidence":0.9,"contribution":0.0007197627730997089}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.7093586824790074},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-1703","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"1703","title":"Choices for Increased Mobility Act of 2026","introducedDate":"2025-02-27","latestActionDate":"2026-07-21","latestActionText":"Received in the Senate and Read twice and referred to the Committee on Finance.","policyArea":"Health","subjects":["Aging","Disability and paralysis","Health care costs and insurance","Health technology, devices, supplies","Medicare"],"sponsorBioguideId":"J000302","cosponsorCount":10,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/1703","industries":[{"industry":"pharma","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"The bill directly affects medical device manufacturers who produce ultralightweight manual wheelchairs by creating new billing codes and payment structures for their products."},{"industry":"insurance","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"The bill changes Medicare payment and billing procedures for durable medical equipment, affecting how insurers process claims for these wheelchairs."},{"industry":"health-providers","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"Medical equipment suppliers who provide wheelchairs to Medicare beneficiaries will face new billing requirements and must potentially inform patients of cost differences."}],"plainSummary":"This bill requires Medicare to create separate billing codes for ultralightweight manual wheelchairs based on the materials used in their construction, with at least one code for wheelchairs made with titanium or carbon fiber and one for those without. Suppliers would continue to receive the standard Medicare payment, but could bill patients directly for any difference between that payment and the actual cost of the wheelchair. Medicare may require suppliers to inform patients about potential out-of-pocket costs before purchase.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"People on Medicare. Anyone who pays a medical bill.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"B001260","score":0.06427617152344335},"overlapCount":9},"meaning":{"percentile":38.951192544621705,"median":0.010636331736930488,"n":6331,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.7093586824790074}},{"billId":"119-hr-6610","bioguideId":"B001327","cycle":2026,"score":0.007621872930590025,"matches":[{"industry":"insurance","donorAmount":45500,"donorShare":0.013893602620136806,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.005176048034952928},{"industry":"legal","donorAmount":21000,"donorShare":0.006412431978524679,"billConfidence":0.75,"contribution":0.0018860094054484351},{"industry":"pharma","donorAmount":5500,"donorShare":0.0016794464705659875,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.0005598154901886625}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.7093586824790074},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-6610","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"6610","title":"Pharmacists Fight Back [in Federal Employee Health Benefit Plans Act]","introducedDate":"2025-12-11","latestActionDate":"2026-07-22","latestActionText":"Ordered to be Reported (Amended) by the Yeas and Nays: 40 - 2.","policyArea":"Health","subjects":["Civil actions and liability","Government employee pay, benefits, personnel management","Health care costs and insurance","Judicial review and appeals","Prescription drugs","User charges and fees"],"sponsorBioguideId":"A000148","cosponsorCount":39,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/6610","industries":[{"industry":"insurance","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"Pharmacy benefit managers, which this bill targets with new liability and judicial review, are typically part of or contracted by health insurers managing federal employee drug benefits."},{"industry":"pharma","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill directly affects pharmacists and the terms under which they dispense prescription drugs, altering the contractual and fee structure in the federal employee drug supply chain."},{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.75,"rationale":"The bill creates new federal civil causes of action and judicial review procedures, which would generate litigation between pharmacists and PBMs."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would allow pharmacists to sue pharmacy benefit managers (PBMs) in federal court if they believe PBM fees or contract terms are unfair, and would let courts review and potentially overturn those fees. The bill applies specifically to prescription drug plans offered to federal employees and retirees. It creates a new legal pathway for pharmacists to challenge the business practices of the middlemen who manage drug benefits.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":